#!/bin/sh
# roost-argv-ext — print this extension's own argv, one argument per line.
#
# A TEST FIXTURE. `roost status` takes no arguments, so the conformance
# fixture beside this one proves nothing about argument fidelity — and a seam
# that hands one process's arguments to another breaks on QUOTING far more
# often than on logic. The dispatcher's job here is small and exact: drop the
# subcommand and pass the rest through unchanged, so `roost argv-ext %200`
# reaches this program as `%200`, the argument it would have had if the user
# had run this binary directly.
#
# The four arguments the suite sends are each chosen against a way that
# breaks: `%200` for a shell that globs or expands it, `sess:win` for a
# splitter that treats a colon as a field separator, `--force` for anything
# upstream that tries to parse options out of an extension's arguments, and
# an argument containing spaces for the one that gets lost every time — a
# `$*` or an unquoted `$@` anywhere on the path would deliver it as four.
#
# `printf '%s\n' "$@"`, not `echo`: an argument of `-n` or `-e` is data here,
# and echo would eat it. One line per argument rather than a single joined
# line for the same reason the arguments were chosen — a joined line cannot
# tell "a b" apart from "a" "b", which is precisely the failure being tested.
#
# /bin/sh, not bash: nothing here needs more, and an extension is any
# executable the author chose to write. That the seam runs one is part of
# what is being asserted.
printf '%s\n' "$@"
